Z||000000||DavidHenshaw||03-23-2001||06:02 PM||||Neither of the doors on my 86 GT have the large spring up near the hinge which is supposed to keep the door from swinging open wildly. Is there a problem keeping the spring seated or is it just a quirk that my car doesn't have them. <P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-23-2001||06:11 PM||||They broke and fell out...||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||03-23-2001||06:20 PM||||if you look on the top of the bottom hinge there should be a little lever that is pressed up against the door by the spring when the door is open.<P>Try your local pontiac dealer for new springs, and have someone help you put them in.  They can be a real pain, and make sure you were safety glasses, I had one come wizzing out very close to my head!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-23-2001||07:28 PM||||there is a tools for this. makes it very easy. It's less than $20. you might could borrow one from some some parts places. One source is in my cave.||||reg||1
Z||000004||varnster||03-23-2001||08:31 PM||||if your to cheap to buy the tool,heres a <BR>trick get A VISE and some heavy duty zip ties<BR>compress the spring in the vise ziptie it, <BR>install the spring cut the ties DONE. WEAR<BR>SAFETY GLASSES, AGAIN WEAR SAFETY GLASSES,<BR>if the zip tie breaks before it is installed<BR>the spring goes flying.and the spring doesnt<BR>care whats in the way. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||03-23-2001||09:45 PM||||I've got a complete lower door hinge with the spring still in. I don't know what side it is for, would have to check if interested. Let me know if you're interested in it. Won't cost much.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||03-23-2001||10:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by varnster:<BR><B>if your to cheap to buy the tool,heres a <BR>trick get A VISE and some heavy duty zip ties<BR>compress the spring in the vise ziptie it, <BR>install the spring cut the ties DONE. WEAR<BR>SAFETY GLASSES, AGAIN WEAR SAFETY GLASSES,<BR>if the zip tie breaks before it is installed<BR>the spring goes flying.and the spring doesnt<BR>care whats in the way. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That's a really good idea!  I will have to try it next time I'm messing around with springs, I bet it would work with ebrake return springs on the rear calipers too, those were a pain.  Springs in general are a pain!<BR><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||03-23-2001||11:06 PM||||I hate that method myself. extremely dangerous. <P>If you are going to try that use steel wire, NOT plastic ties. That spring compresses at something like 75-100 pounds per inch. <P>Like I said above.... you may be able to borow this tool from some of the parts stores. some of the chain stores lend tools with a deposit.||||reg||4
